seg-333a1114746a = Optical PNT through the atmosphere

# text
# c1: optical-atmosphere
seg-47118f54f060 = The <c1/> scenario kind answers how often an optical link to the ground works, why it fails, what margin or technique would help, and what a clock does while it is down.

# text
seg-06db0ea8f428 = Kshana is the analysis side only. It does not run an optical-ground-station experiment, and it names no measurement partner. The kind is <b>MODELLED</b>: its numerics are checked against an independent program, but no measured availability record has been compared, and every weather, technique and clock input is an assumption. It is an advisory analysis, not a certified availability, integrity or navigation product.

# text
seg-83c16fd89005 = Both bundled scenarios carry ILLUSTRATIVE cloud climates. They are inputs, not measurements and not any dataset's values.

# attr:alt
seg-e9dbbd9cfa32 = One seeded realisation of the link margin of the site 'northern' over { "336" } h, from a real run of engine v{ $version } on scenarios/optical-atmosphere-network.toml (seed { "37" }, a step of { "600" } s). The margin falls below zero during cloud episodes; in this draw the link is up { "40.5" } % of the time, with { "27" } outages and a longest outage of { "30.7" } h. The inputs are illustrative, the series is one draw from the model and not the climate, and it differs from the statistical availability ({ "37.8" } %) by the sampling error of the draw. Advisory analysis only: not a certified availability, integrity or navigation product, and not a prediction for any real site unless its climate inputs come from a stated dataset.

# text
seg-13a52dfbd8d1 = What is computed

# text
# c1: atmospheric_loss_db
# c2: optical_linkbudget
seg-46fbaf0c6a12 = <b>Clear-air loss</b> at the wavelength and elevation: aerosol extinction from the Kim, McArthur and Korevaar visibility law, exponential in height, plus Rayleigh scattering and a stated gas-absorption input. This replaces the single lumped <c1/> of <c2/> when an atmosphere is supplied. Without one, the link budget is bit-identical to { "0.36" } (pre-registered bar A5).

# text
# c1: Cn²
seg-929c80326b41 = <b>Turbulence.</b> A Hufnagel–Valley <c1/> profile (HV 5/7 by default) integrated along the slant path gives the plane- or spherical-wave Rytov variance, uplink or downlink. From it come the Andrews–Phillips scintillation index, the gamma-gamma parameters, the weak-turbulence aperture-averaging factor (Churnside) and the gamma-gamma fade probability and fade margin. Aperture averaging is applied by moment matching: the scintillation index is scaled by the averaging factor and both log variances are rescaled to give it exactly.

# text
# c1: τ = 3L/(2 ρ_w r_e)
seg-3fb7be7ef95d = <b>Cloud.</b> A site's cloud climate is the ITU-R P.840 form: a probability of cloud and, given cloud, a log-normal liquid-water column. P.840 supplies the amount of cloud. The attenuation is the geometric-optics optical depth <c1/>, because P.840's own attenuation law is a microwave law and does not apply at { "0.85" } to { "1.55" } µm, where droplets are far larger than the wavelength.

# text
# c1: null
seg-18311430b2a3 = <b>Availability</b> of a site: the link is up while the cloud and turbulence fades together stay inside the margin left after the clear-air loss. Cloud and turbulence are taken as independent. The report gives the availability, the clear-sky availability, the <b>ceiling</b> no margin can lift (an optical link cannot pass thick cloud), and the extra margin needed for each target availability, or <c1/> if the target is above the ceiling.

# text
seg-885e06050a9a = <b>Outages.</b> Durations are log-normal with a stated mean and spread. The report gives the outages per year, the duration at the median, 90th and 99th percentile, and the probability and share of outage time beyond { "1" }, { "6" }, { "24" } and { "72" } hours.

# text
# c1: N
# c2: 1 − Π(1−a_i)
# c3: exp(−d/d₀)
# c4: d₀ → 0
seg-4b9b6fefad6b = <b>Site diversity.</b> The best <c1/> sites combined with independent weather (<c2/>) and with distance-correlated weather: pair correlation <c3/>, combined in a one-factor Gaussian copula. At <c4/> it is the independent union, and at full correlation the best single site.

# text
# c1: [[technique]]
seg-b85681b9fd93 = <b>Techniques.</b> Each <c1/> is a what-if: a new wavelength (the clear-sky budget, the clear-air loss and the aperture averaging are re-evaluated), a new receive aperture, a stated fractional scintillation reduction (adaptive optics) and extra margin. The result is the availability gain over the baseline at the same site. Kshana does not model an adaptive-optics loop, so the gains are inputs.

# text
# c1: [clock]
# c2: A + U·E[min(D, T_h)]/E[D]
# c3: holdover.rs
seg-a59d07280524 = <b>Timing and ranging through an outage.</b> Each outage duration is mapped through the closed-form coast variance of the clock holdover model, for the clocks listed in <c1/>. The report gives the timing and ranging error at stated outage durations, the holdover to the timing threshold, the share of outages inside it, and the timing availability <c2/>. The class power-law floors are the holdover module's synthesised ones, so a tight threshold is governed by that assumed floor, as <c3/> states.

# text
seg-59774a9e8685 = Link margin against time ({ "0.38" })

# text
# c1: [timeseries]
# c2: duration_h
# c3: step_s
# c4: seed
# c5: p_cloud
seg-fcfdd2e9e0ad = A <c1/> table (<c2/> up to { "8760" }, <c3/>, <c4/>) adds to each site one <b>seeded realisation</b> of the model above: the line of sight alternates between clear spells (exponential, mean set so the share of time in cloud is the stated <c5/>) and cloud outages (log-normal, the stated mean and spread); each outage draws one liquid-water column from the stated climate and holds it; each step draws one gamma-gamma scintillation sample (independent from step to step, a stated simplification) and the terminal is on target with the stated pointing availability. The step's margin is the fade budget minus the cloud and scintillation fades, and the link is up when the margin is not negative and the terminal is on target, the condition the availability integral uses.

